92186045417] [17592186417601 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417601 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f999aae1-a9f0-58f9-8bee-410f830f1fb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417602 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=0,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417602 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={0,2,5}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(0,2),(5,0),(5,2)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}}\\,,\n$$\n\n \nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a2bda0c-9e42-53f1-a525-c0492f5a91dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417603 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${0,2,5}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(0,2)\\in R$ and $(2,0)\\notin R$\n\n- $(5,0)\\in R$ and $(0,5)\\notin R$\n\n- $(5,2)\\in R$ and $(2,5)\\notin R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ in the relation. So, for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{antisymmetric}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $Z$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "452336ac-2206-5d26-872d-718b80ac9d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417604 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, for any integers $x$ and $y,$ if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2),$ then $y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2),\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{symmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not antisymmetric since, for example, $2≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ and $6≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ but $2\\ne 6.$" #edb/ref 17592186