n(3,6)\\notin R,\\,(6,9)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cells corresponding to these elements must all be $0.$\n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $9$ |  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nWe do not have enough information to determine whether $(9,3)$ is an element of $R.$ \n\n|  | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $9$ | $?$ | $1$ | $1$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f3b0fa8-024e-5898-b1f6-7de09c79b487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417606 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, for any real numbers $x$ and $y,$ if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ then ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{symmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not antisymmetric since, for example, ${1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=0$ and $(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=0$ but $1\\ne -1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={5,10,15}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(5,5),(5,10),(10,5)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,{{field-1}},\n$$\n\n \nthe relation is \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83135e57-d72a-5f9f-8787-4bc48f405724" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417607 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${5,10,15}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(5,10)\\in R$ and $(10,5)\\in R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ in the relation. So, for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{symmetric}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417608 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417608 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417608 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417608 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417608 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417608 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417609 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417609 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417609 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417609 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417609 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={-1,0,2}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(-1,2),(2,-1),(0,0),(0,2)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}}\nand\n{{field-5}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-6}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b24ff238-78b5-5737-b5c9-c38496b2d157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417610 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${0,1,2}.$