ws pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWe are given elements of the adjacency matrix in the following highlighted cells outside the diagonal:  \n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ |  | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  |\n| $8$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAs a result, the relation contains the pair $(8,2).$ Since $R$ must be symmetric, we require \n\n$$\n(2,8)\\in R.\n$$\n\nSo, the cell corresponding to this element must be equal to $1.$\n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ |  | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  | $1$ |\n| $8$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nAlso, note the following:\n\n- The symmetric relation condition is equivalent to $(x,y)\\notin R⟹(y,x)\\notin R.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n(-4,8)\\notin R⟹(8,-4)\\notin R,\\,\\text{and}\\,(2,-4)\\notin R⟹(-4,2)\\notin R.\n$$\n\n- We do not have enough information to determine whether $(2,2)$ is an element of $R.$\n\nLet's add this information to our table.\n\n|  | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $?$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417612 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417612 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417612 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417612 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/dac9030db59b2b51b2b95c8bc9b5988106be8d198b7f947d33c8678d1cfa3b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417612 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd780cfc-a5dd-5c8f-a2c7-e44b9d2a3075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417612 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $R$ be a relation defined on the set $R$ as follows: \n\n$x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\le y$ \n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⟹\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceafef3e-b20d-5064-9135-0687709767da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417613 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nThe relation $R$ is antisymmetric. Indeed, for any real numbers $x$ and $y,$ if $x\\le y$ and $y\\le x,$ then $x$ and $y$ must be the same number.\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\nx\\le y\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\le x\\,⟹\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nthe relation is $\\text{antisymmetric}.$\n\n**Note:** $R$ is not symmetric since, for example, $0\\le 1$ but $1≰0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a relation $R$ that is defined on the set $A={2,4,6}.$ Given that $R$ is symmetric, enter the missing values in the adjacency matrix. \n\n**Note**: If there is not enough information to determine a particular cell's value, then select the question mark $(\\text{?}).$\n\n|  | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $6$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $0$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417614 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4393a9f-4375-593